 transfer to presidential voting"], "rationale": "With no polls, ratings, or markets for this race, the forecast rests entirely on Iowa's six-point Republican partisan lean, which points to a Republican win but not a safe one. Headlines about Democratic strength in Republican-territory governor races are suggestive but not specific enough to shift the number.", "watch": "Once 2028 nominees are set and Iowa polling or expert ratings appear, candidate quality could move this several points in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Iowa sits about six points to the right of the nation in presidential voting, and with the incumbent term-limited, the 2028 race is fully open. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices are available yet, so the state's partisan lean is doing nearly all of the forecasting work. That lean favors Republicans, but not overwhelmingly.\n\nThe broader environment is unsettled in ways that cut both directions. Democrats have polled well in governor races deep in Republican territory, and both parties' nominations remain contested, with Republicans already jockeying against a frontrunner and several Democrats eyeing runs. Iowa's loss of its first-in-the-nation primary status matters little for the general election itself. The race rates as a Republican lean with wide uncertainty, and early signals should be treated as provisional.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2447, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.291, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2906, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2896, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2832, "margin": -6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2863, "marg